@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent file, project root, load error, general agent, model tier,
  routable tier, medium tier,
  tier word,
}
@design/claude-mcp-server.sigil from ClaudeMcpServer import {
  readiness marker, plugin data folder, SessionStart hook, delegate tool,
  await tool, tasks tool, deferred line, task mode, MCP task,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import { parent adapter, parent host, child host }
@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import {
  brief text, queued task, result text, completion notice,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import { parent session id, store folder }
@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import { task id, start time, child driver }

component ClaudeRoutingHooks {
  goal {
    Let a developer running compound-engineering skills in Claude Code send
    each generic subagent of a configured model tier to subzero instead of
    Claude's own Agent tool, through text added at session start and a hook
    that redirects a spawn that slips past, without editing any skill and
    without ever stranding Claude when subzero cannot serve the call.
  }

  state {
    The *routing settings* are the part of the agent file that holds a
    *hook switch*, which is on unless it is set to false, and an optional
    *generic agent list* of agent type names. ClaudeRoutingHooks reads them
    from the agent file
    that AgentConfig loads for it, and no other component reads them.

    A *compound-engineering skill* names the model of a generic subagent with a
    tier word, which AgentConfig maps to a model tier.

    An *Agent spawn* is one call of Claude Code's *Agent tool*, named Agent or
    Task, with its prompt, a short description, an optional *subagent type*, an
    optional *model alias*, and an optional isolation or fork request. The
    *hook input* is the JSON that Claude Code gives a hook process. It
    carries the fields of an Agent spawn and the parent session id.

    The *alias table* maps an Agent spawn with no model alias to the
    model tier large, haiku to small and sonnet to medium, and it maps neither
    opus nor fable.

    A *generic spawn* is an Agent spawn whose subagent type is absent or is
    on the generic agent list. That list is general-purpose and claude unless
    the routing settings give one, and a list that the routing settings give
    replaces it.

    A *pass-back* is an Agent spawn whose short description begins with the
    word
    passback. Claude uses it to send a subagent back to its own Agent tool when
    the
    delegate tool is refused or returns a tool error and made no task. A
    delegate
    call that returned a task id stays with subzero, even when its result is
    failed, and is never passed back.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks is a parent adapter of the Claude Code parent host that
    reaches the core only
    through AgentConfig. Its hook processes run no Kernel, register no
    parent adapter and import no child driver.

    The *routing text* is what the SessionStart hook adds to Claude's context.
    The *deny memory* is one folder for each parent session id under the
    plugin data folder. It holds one entry for each prompt that the hook
    denied, named by a hash of that prompt and keeping the model tier it was
    denied toward.

    The *deny reason* is the text Claude sees when the hook denies an
    Agent spawn.

    A *wake marker* is a plugin data folder entry for one background task of a
    parent session id. The *wake hook* is an asyncRewake Stop hook that watches
    only those entries, never the store folder.
  }

  logic {
    When a Stop event starts the wake hook, it waits while a wake marker of
    that parent session id is pending. When a marker records a result text and
    completion notice, the hook atomically claims it and exits with both, waking
    Claude. If no marker remains, it exits without waking Claude. Competing
    wake hooks cannot claim the same marker. Before the hook's timeout, when
    work is still pending, it wakes Claude with a reminder to keep waiting; the
    next Stop event starts a fresh wake hook for that work.

    At each session start, after the parent session id is recorded, read the
    agent file under the project root that CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR names. When no
    project root is given or the agent file is missing, add nothing and end
    the flow. When the agent file exists and fails with a load error, add a
    one-line warning that routing is off as the routing text, which ends the
    flow. When it loads with no routable tier that a tier word maps to, add
    nothing
    and end the flow. Otherwise build the routing text from each routable tier
    that
    a tier word maps to and add it to
    Claude's context, which ends the flow.

    On an Agent spawn, allow it and end the flow when the hook switch of the
    routing settings is off or the agent file fails to load. Then allow it and
    end the flow when no readiness marker names a live process whose recorded
    parent process is one of the hook's own ancestor processes. Then allow it
    and
    end the flow when it asks for a fork or sets isolation. Then allow it and
    end
    the flow when it is a pass-back and the deny memory of the
    parent session id
    holds the hash of its prompt, whatever model tier that entry keeps. Then,
    when
    the
    deny memory of the parent session id holds the hash of its prompt and the
    model tier that entry keeps is still a routable tier, deny it with the
    deny reason toward that model tier, which ends the flow. Then allow it and
    end
    the
    flow when it is not a generic spawn, or when
    the alias table maps it to no routable tier. Otherwise record its prompt in
    the deny memory with that model tier, and deny it with the deny reason
    toward that model tier, which ends the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    The routing text must name each routable tier that a tier word maps to
    against
    its tier word, say
    that this work goes to the delegate tool, by its full name
    mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__delegate, with that model tier instead of the
    Agent tool, and list every other tier word as
    staying on Claude. It must say that a subagent spawn that asks for a fork
    or for worktree isolation stays on the Agent tool and is never sent to the
    delegate tool. While the task mode in the readiness marker is off, it must
    tell
    Claude to dispatch every routed subagent
    with wait false in one message and then call the await tool with every
    task id
    and up to 600 seconds until every task is done. A non-interactive server
    waits even for wait false and returns the result in that delegate call.
    While the task mode is on,
    it
    must tell Claude to dispatch every routed subagent in one message without
    asking to wait, so that each becomes an MCP task, and to wait for every
    completion notice before it uses the results together. While the
    readiness marker is absent or holds no task mode, it must tell Claude to
    dispatch every
    routed subagent in one message without asking to wait, and to collect a
    task id
    that comes back with the await tool, so that the wording works in both
    modes. It must say that a
    queued task has not launched yet and that a task's launch time is its
    start time, and that when the delegate tool is refused or returns a
    tool error
    and made no task it must pass the subagent back through a pass-back, and
    that
    once a task id came back the work stays with subzero. It must say, in one
    sentence, that a task whose result an await reply does not show, which the
    await tool gives as a deferred line, must be read with the tasks tool
    before it is used.

    The routing text must stay far below the 10,000-character limit that Claude
    Code puts on added context, because it is added again at every session
    start, resume, /clear and compaction.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks must add the routing text only through the output of the
    SessionStart hook and must never write it to CLAUDE.md or any other file
    of the project.

    The deny reason must name the delegate tool by its full name
    mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__delegate, the model tier as tier, the prompt
    unchanged as the brief text, and wait false while the task mode is off, the
    same neutral wording as the routing text while the readiness marker holds
    no task mode,
    and it must say that this is a routing rule and not an error, and that
    Claude
    must not call Agent again for this subagent or change its model, except
    that
    when the delegate tool is refused or returns a tool error and made no task
    it
    must call Agent again with the prompt unchanged and a short description
    that
    begins with passback.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks must deny only a generic spawn that the alias table maps
    to
    a
    routable tier, or an Agent spawn whose prompt the deny memory holds with a
    model tier that is still a routable tier and that is not a pass-back, and
    must
    allow every other
    Agent spawn, including a spawn of a named
    specialist agent whose prompt the deny memory does not hold.

    An Agent spawn that asks for a fork or sets isolation must always be
    allowed, before the deny memory is read, because a subzero child starts in
    the shared checkout and sees only its brief text. A fork is a spawn whose
    agent type is fork, and any isolation value that is not empty counts, such
    as
    worktree or remote, including a value this design does not know, because
    subzero can honor no isolated spawn. These are the shapes of a real
    hook input
    that Claude Code 2.1.318 gave on 2026-11-10: an Agent tool input
    with a description, a prompt, an optional model, run in background, and
    isolation worktree or agent type fork when asked.

    A pass-back whose prompt the deny memory of its parent session id holds
    must
    always be allowed, before the deny memory can deny it, so that a subagent
    whose
    delegation is refused or returns a tool error can still run on Claude's own
    Agent tool and Claude is never stranded. A pass-back whose prompt the
    deny memory does not hold gets no exemption and goes through the rest of
    the
    flow
    like any other Agent spawn, because the hook cannot tell whether the
    delegate tool failed and a pass-back of a prompt that was never denied
    would
    otherwise
    be a way around the routing. This is the one deliberate exception to the
    rule
    that a prompt that was denied is denied again.

    The alias table must not map opus or fable, because the hook cannot tell
    them from the parent model, but an Agent spawn with either alias whose
    prompt the deny memory holds must still be denied, while the model tier
    that entry keeps is a routable tier.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks must deny only while a readiness marker names a live
    process
    whose recorded parent process is one of the hook's own ancestor processes,
    so that two Claude sessions on one project never remove each other's
    readiness and a session whose own server is down never relies on the
    server of another session.

    Any error inside a hook, a missing or malformed hook input, or a
    deny memory that cannot be read must allow the Agent spawn: the hook exits
    0 with no output. The SessionStart hook must likewise exit 0 and add
    nothing on any error other than a load error of an agent file that
    exists.

    Each denied prompt must be recorded in the deny memory as its own
    create-only entry, and no write may read or replace another entry, so that
    the hook processes of one concurrent batch never lose each other's entries.
    A prompt denied under one parent session id must not be remembered under
    another, and each new entry deletes deny memory folders older than seven
    days, except the folder of the parent session id that is writing. An entry
    keeps the model tier it was first denied toward and is never replaced: when
    its tier stops being a routable tier and the same prompt is later denied
    toward
    another model tier by the alias table, the entry still holds the first
    tier,
    and a retry whose model has no alias is then allowed until the entry's tier
    is
    routable again. That bounded hole is accepted, because replacing an entry
    would break the rule that no write may replace another entry.

    When the hook switch is off, every Agent spawn must be allowed while the
    routing text is still added.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks reads the agent file under the project root that
    *CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR* names, as the one named exception to the rule that
    only
    DelegationService resolves a project root, because a hook process starts no
    DelegationService.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks keeps its logic in one importable module with two thin
    entry
    scripts. The SessionStart hook runs the recording of the parent session id
    that ClaudeMcpServer owns, and the *PreToolUse hook* matches Agent|Task so
    that older Claude Code builds are covered.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks must route by the tier word through the routing text and
    the
    alias table and not skill by skill, and must not need any change to a
    compound-engineering skill.

    ClaudeRoutingHooks depends on AgentConfig for the agent file and the
    routable tier names, reads the routing settings from that agent file
    itself, and on ClaudeMcpServer for the
    readiness marker,
    the plugin data folder, the SessionStart hook and the delegate tool.
  }

  cases {
    In every case here the agent file also holds the medium tier and a
    general agent, and a case that names the model tiers configured names only
    the
    tiers
    that carry a tier word.

    At a SessionStart with no readiness marker, the routing text tells Claude
    to
    dispatch without asking to wait and to collect any task id with the
    await tool,
    and the next SessionStart after the server wrote its task mode uses the
    wording of that task mode.

    With only the model tier medium configured, the routing text sends mid-tier
    work to delegate with the model tier medium and says that cheapest capable
    and parent model work stays on Claude, and that a spawn which asks for a
    fork or for worktree isolation stays on the Agent tool.

    With the model tier large configured, a generic spawn with no model alias
    from compound-engineering or from the user's own work is denied toward
    large.

    With no model tier configured, no routing text is added and every
    Agent spawn is allowed. In a project with no agent file the SessionStart hook
    prints nothing and exits 0, and with a malformed agent file it adds the
    one-line warning and every Agent spawn is allowed.

    With medium configured and a live readiness marker, a generic spawn with
    the alias sonnet is denied with a deny reason that names the delegate tool,
    the model tier medium and wait false.

    With the model tier medium configured, an Agent spawn of the named
    specialist agent Explore with sonnet is allowed.

    With medium configured and no readiness marker, or one whose process is
    dead, or one whose recorded parent process is not an ancestor of the hook,
    a generic spawn with sonnet is allowed and the review finishes on Claude.

    With only medium configured, after a generic spawn with sonnet is denied,
    the same prompt with no model alias and then with haiku is denied again
    with the same deny reason, and a different prompt with haiku is allowed.

    With the model tier medium configured, an Agent spawn with isolation set to
    worktree and the alias sonnet is allowed, and so is a fork.

    With the model tier medium configured, after a generic spawn with sonnet is
    denied and the delegate tool is refused, the same prompt as a pass-back,
    whose
    short description begins with passback, is allowed. A pass-back with a
    prompt
    that was never denied, from a generic spawn that the alias table maps to a
    routable tier, is denied like any other spawn and recorded in the
    deny memory.

    A file whose only model tier is the medium tier routes mid-tier work to it
    with
    the delegate tool and leaves every other generic spawn on Claude, because
    only
    the tier word mid-tier maps to a configured tier.

    Six hook processes run at once and deny six different prompts, and every
    one
    of the six is denied again on retry, because each has its own entry in the
    deny memory.

    With all three model tier entries configured, the routing text stays under
    3,000 characters.
  }

  interface {
    The wake hook reads each wake marker of the current parent session id and
    delivers its result text and completion notice when it is complete.

    Add the routing text to Claude's context at each session start, resume,
    /clear and compaction, built from the agent file under the project root
    that CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR names, read at that moment, or a
    one-line warning when an agent file that exists fails to load, or nothing
    when there is no agent file or no routable tier that a tier word maps to.

    Answer each Agent spawn of the Agent tool through the PreToolUse hook with
    allow, or with deny and the deny reason that Claude sees, as its logic
    gives,
    and allow the pass-back that its logic names.
  }

  decisions {
    Instructions alone would let Claude slip back to its own Agent tool and
    lose coverage without a signal, and a native shim agent would start the
    reviewer clock at the Agent call and bring the queue problem back, so the
    routing text comes with a redirecting hook. The hook fails open because a
    down server would otherwise block every reviewer, and it remembers what it
    denied so that a changed model alias cannot route around it. The hook
    checks no
    child host, because its process has no registered child driver and so
    cannot
    tell a
    supported child host from another: an agent file with a child host that is
    not
    supported
    shows up as a tool error of the delegate tool, and the pass-back answers
    it.
    Worktree and
    fork spawns stay on Claude until delegate has a worktree mode. The
    routing text
    is added at session start and not written to CLAUDE.md so that no
    committed file changes, nothing goes stale when the model tier set
    changes, and nothing is left behind when the plugin is removed.

    The general agent runs with write and shell tools inside its child host,
    outside the permission prompts of the Claude Code parent host, which the
    user
    accepted so that compound-engineering reviewers keep full fidelity. The
    routing text reads the task mode from the readiness marker because a hook
    process cannot see what Claude Code advertised to the server.

    Two limits are known and left open. The hook input carries the permission
    mode of the session, which the hook does not read, so a generic spawn made
    in plan mode is still sent to a child with write and shell tools. The
    SessionStart hook runs before the server connects, so when the server then
    fails to start the routing text still says that routing is on; nothing is
    denied, because no readiness marker exists, and Claude keeps its own
    Agent tool.
  }
}
